# P49 — substantive answers to João's 4 Oct comments: patient link kept on the cross-border path and argued
# necessary (Option A, Tiger 5 Oct; EHDS Art 9(1)-(2), verified on a mirror of the OJ text); payload defined
# by source and necessity argued per class, with Corollary 2.3 (sensitivity); Art 17(3)(d) and the Art 71
# opt-out; Semantics I anchored in the OpenNCP source and the Danish 9.1.0 deployment schema (inspected
# 5 Oct, commits c8a82d8 and 61094e6); redactable/chameleon-hash prior art; unimplemented parts named in
# the summaries.
EDITS = [{'id': 'P49-01', 'file': 'Chapter4_TRF_Model_v1.md', 'old': 'Retention of accessible plaintext payload for ten years therefore exceeds what Article 17(3)(b) protects. Article 5(1)(c) positively requires that it not be retained where the purpose can be achieved without it.', 'new': 'Retention of accessible plaintext payload for the length of the floor therefore exceeds what Article 17(3)(b) protects. Article 5(1)(c) positively requires that it not be retained where the purpose can be achieved without it.\n\n*Per record class.* The argument has to be made for each class separately, because the payload is defined by where it comes from (Section 4.3), not by whether it is needed. For a cross-border record it is specific: the accountability question is which clinician accessed which patient\'s record, under which assertion and with what outcome, and the accessor fields with the patient link answer it; the patient\'s name and birth date as typed into the query add nothing to that answer. For a Secure Processing Environment record it is weaker. Article 73(1)(e) asks for logs sufficient "to verify and audit all processing operations in that environment", and an auditor checking that an extraction stayed within its permit may need to know what was run. The thesis therefore does not assume that the whole activity content is unnecessary there; Corollary 2.3 states what follows if part of it is needed.\n\n*The patient link.* The link from an access event to the person whose data were accessed is not payload: it is the other half of the accountability question. The Regulation gives natural persons the right to obtain information on any access to their personal electronic health data through the health professional access service, including "information on the healthcare provider or other individuals who accessed the personal electronic health data", "the date and time of access" and "which personal electronic health data were accessed", available "for at least three years from each date of access to the data" (Article 9(1)–(2) EHDS). That information cannot be produced from a record that no longer says whose data were accessed. Retaining the link is therefore necessary for compliance with a legal obligation within the meaning of Article 17(3)(b), and an erasure request reaches the payload but not the link, at least for the period Article 9(2) fixes. Article 9 is framed around the health professional access service, and whether every cross-border access event routed through a National Contact Point falls within it is a question of how the access is made; the patient-facing audit-log interface of MyHealth@EU (Chapter 1, Section 1.2) serves the same purpose for those that do not. Beyond the Article 9(2) period the link is kept for the rest of the floor on the strength of the deployment binding, which is illustrative here (Section 4.3), and it is removed at the terminal transition (Section 4.8). In a Secure Processing Environment the position differs, because the ceiling may reach the link (Section 4.6).', 'why': 'João M2: Option A, with Art 9 EHDS; M1: necessity per class'}, {'id': 'P49-02', 'file': 'Chapter4_TRF_Model_v1.md', 'old': '- $p(a)$ is the *personal-data payload*: the personal data the record carries beyond what accountability requires (Section 4.2). Its content depends on the record class.', 'new': '- $p(a)$ is the *payload*: the content the record carries about what was accessed, as distinct from who accessed it, when and under what authority. It is defined by its source, which depends on the record class; whether accountability needs it is argued per class in Section 4.2, and Corollary 2.3 states what follows if part of it is needed.', 'why': 'João M1: no circular definition'}, {'id': 'P49-03', 'file': 'Chapter4_TRF_Model_v1.md', 'old': 'whatever an environment records under those headings beyond the accessor fields is $p(a)$.', 'new': 'what an environment records under those headings beyond the accessor fields is $p(a)$, and whether auditing "all processing operations" needs part of it is the question Corollary 2.3 answers conditionally.', 'why': 'João M1'}, {'id': 'P49-04', 'file': 'Chapter4_TRF_Model_v1.md', 'old': 'The model does not require $p(a)$ to be a clinical document, only personal data that the accountability purpose does not need.', 'new': 'The model does not require $p(a)$ to be a clinical document.', 'why': 'João M1'}, {'id': 'P49-05', 'file': 'Chapter4_TRF_Model_v1.md', 'old': 'The model does not represent the Article 17(3) exemptions under which a controller may lawfully refuse, and so treats every arriving request as valid; Chapter 3, Section 3.7 explains why this affects the frequencies reported in Chapter 5 but not the propositions.', 'new': 'The model does not represent the Article 17(3) exemptions under which a controller may lawfully refuse, and so treats every arriving request as valid; Chapter 3, Section 3.7 explains why this affects the frequencies reported in Chapter 5 but not the propositions. In the secondary-use pathway two provisions narrow C2 further. Article 17(3)(d) GDPR disapplies the right where processing is necessary for "scientific or historical research purposes or statistical purposes in accordance with Article 89(1) in so far as the right referred to in paragraph 1 is likely to render impossible or seriously impair the achievement of the objectives of that processing", and the Regulation gives natural persons instead a right to opt out of secondary use, which is reversible and does not reach processing under data permits issued before it was exercised (Article 71(1) and (3) EHDS). For the environment\'s own access logs, which serve Article 73(1)(e) rather than the research, the exemption that applies within the floor is Article 17(3)(b). The effect is that fewer SPE requests reach C2 than the model assumes, and a record that no request reaches faces only the floor and the ceiling, which is the case of Corollary 1.2.', 'why': 'João M5: Art 17(3)(d), Art 71'}, {'id': 'P49-06', 'file': 'Chapter4_TRF_Model_v1.md', 'old': "How common such logs are is not established here. What is established is that nothing in the instruments rules them out and that the cross-border profile supplies their content, since ITI-55 requires both gateways to record the patient's demographic query (Section 4.3).", 'new': "The reference implementation of the cross-border gateway keeps such a log. OpenNCP's audit record repository stores every received audit message whole, in a database column for the message content, with the patient identifier in a readable column and a further column for the participant-object query; it refuses to modify a stored message, and it contains no retention, purge or erasure logic, so how long the content stays readable is left to the operator (OpenNCP Project, 2017; Sundhedsdatastyrelsen, 2026). In the 2017 source inspected, the patient-discovery audit carries the patient identifier and the full SOAP security header of the request, though not the demographic query that ITI-55 asks gateways to record; the current schema, deployed with OpenNCP 9.1.0, keeps the column for it. How other deployments configure retention is not established here.", 'why': 'João M6: Semantics I anchored in OpenNCP'}, {'id': 'P49-07', 'file': 'Chapter4_TRF_Model_v1.md', 'old': 'The model is tri-lateral in constraint type and indifferent to the number of floor instances.', 'new': 'The model is tri-lateral in constraint type and indifferent to the number of floor instances.\n\n*Corollary 2.3 (sensitivity to what auditing needs).* Partition the payload as $p(a) = p_n(a) \\cup p_r(a)$, where $p_n(a)$ is any part that auditing turns out to need, and retain $p_n(a)$ with the accessor fields. The trajectory of Proposition 2, with $p_r(a)$ in place of $p(a)$, satisfies C1 and C2, and it satisfies C3 unless $p_n(a)$ is itself electronic health data within the environment on the broad reading of Article 68(12). In that case C1 and C3 collide over $p_n(a)$ for exactly the records that Proposition 3(ii) identifies, and only relocation (Section 4.10, Rival 6) meets both.\n\n*Proof sketch.* Replace $p$ by $p_r$ in steps 1–4 and add $p_n$ to the fields that condition (i) of Semantics II keeps intact. C1 holds as before. C2 holds for $p_r$ as before, and for $p_n$ through Article 17(3)(b), which applies because $p_n$ is by assumption necessary. C3 constrains whatever is electronic health data within the environment: if $p_n$ is not, C3 is met as before; if it is, C3 requires it gone from $t_\\pi(a) + 6$ while C1 requires it until $t(a) + F(a)$, and Proposition 3 gives the records for which these overlap. ∎\n\nFeasibility therefore survives any enlargement of what auditing needs, except an enlargement into data concerning health inside a Secure Processing Environment, where it fails exactly as Corollary 1.2 does and is answered the same way.', 'why': 'João M1: sensitivity result'}, {'id': 'P49-08', 'file': 'Chapter4_TRF_Model_v1.md', 'old': 'and what the log holds is what accountability requires.', 'new': 'and what the log holds is what accountability requires. Redactable and chameleon-hash designs, reviewed in Chapter 2, Section 2.4, resolve the same tension the other way, by letting a key holder rewrite the log.', 'why': 'link to redactable literature'}, {'id': 'P49-09', 'file': 'Chapter4_TRF_Model_v1.md', 'old': 'at the point where the ATNA audit record is written.', 'new': 'at the point where the ATNA audit record is written. The patient link is retained there through the floor, for the reason Section 4.2 gives, and removed at the terminal transition.', 'why': 'Option A carried into §4.8'}, {'id': 'P49-10', 'file': 'Chapter4_TRF_Model_v1.md', 'old': 'is accounted for under the storage-limitation principle.', 'new': 'is accounted for under the storage-limitation principle. The terminal transition, the per-entry nonce, consistency proofs and the accumulator layer are specified but not implemented in the artefact (Chapter 6, Section 6.1).', 'why': 'João: unimplemented parts said in the summary'}, {'id': 'P49-11', 'file': 'Chapter5_Demonstration_v1.md', 'old': 'The link to the patient was removed at the ceiling, and its removal is itself a logged entry.', 'new': 'The link to the patient was removed at the ceiling, and its removal is itself a logged entry; a cross-border record keeps the link through the floor (Chapter 4, Section 4.2).', 'why': 'Option A carried into §5.7'}, {'id': 'P49-12', 'file': 'Chapter7_Conclusion_v1.md', 'old': 'for logs kept at patient-record granularity it removes the subject link at the ceiling.', 'new': "for logs kept at patient-record granularity it removes the subject link at the ceiling, while cross-border records keep it through the floor, because the person's right to learn who accessed their data needs it.", 'why': 'Option A carried into §7.1'}, {'id': 'P49-13', 'file': 'Chapter7_Conclusion_v1.md', 'old': 'At floor expiry it performs a terminal transition that destroys the identifying metadata and leaves a non-identifiable statistical residue.', 'new': 'At floor expiry it performs a terminal transition that destroys the identifying metadata and leaves a non-identifiable statistical residue; that transition is specified, not implemented.', 'why': 'João: unimplemented'}, {'id': 'P49-14', 'file': 'Chapter2_LitReview_v2_FINAL.md', 'old': 'Chapter 4, Section 4.8 keeps the construction and resolves the tension by deciding what an entry may contain.', 'new': "Chapter 4, Section 4.8 keeps the construction and resolves the tension by deciding what an entry may contain.\n\nA second line of work resolves the same tension the other way, by allowing content inside an integrity-protected structure to change. Chameleon hashes are collision-resistant except for the holder of a trapdoor, who can find collisions at will (Krawczyk & Rabin, 1998). Sanitizable signatures let a designated sanitizer replace marked parts of a signed message while the signature still verifies (Ateniese, Chou, de Medeiros, & Tsudik, 2005), and redactable signatures let parts be removed without any key (Johnson, Molnar, Song, & Wagner, 2002). Redactable blockchains link blocks with chameleon hashes so that a trapdoor holder can replace or remove a block's content and the chain still validates, with the right to be forgotten named as a use case (Ateniese, Magri, Venturi, & Andrade, 2017); later designs spread the power to rewrite across attribute-key holders (Derler, Samelin, Slamanig, & Striecks, 2019) or a voting majority (Deuber, Magri, & Thyagarajan, 2019), and the approaches have been surveyed with the GDPR as their main motivation (Politou, Casino, Alepis, & Patsakis, 2021). Each weakens the binding: integrity comes to mean consistent with what the authorised editor allows rather than unchanged since written, and whoever holds the trapdoor, the sanitizer key, the attribute keys or the majority can rewrite history, in the trapdoor schemes invisibly to anyone without an old copy. The mechanism of this thesis takes the opposite route. Its log is never edited; erasable content is kept out of it, and destroyed by destroying keys. The trust that redaction places in an editor is placed instead in key destruction (Chapter 6, Section 6.3).", 'why': 'João: redactable-log prior art'}, {'id': 'P49-15', 'file': 'Chapter2_LitReview_v2_FINAL.md', 'old': 'Bai, T., Hu', 'new': 'Ateniese, G., Chou, D. H., de Medeiros, B., & Tsudik, G. (2005). Sanitizable signatures. In S. de Capitani di Vimercati, P. Syverson, & D. Gollmann (Eds.), *Computer Security – ESORICS 2005* (LNCS 3679, pp. 159–177). Springer. https://doi.org/10.1007/11555827_10\n\nAteniese, G., Magri, B., Venturi, D., & Andrade, E. R. (2017). Redactable blockchain – or – rewriting history in Bitcoin and friends. In *2017 IEEE European Symposium on Security and Privacy (EuroS&P)* (pp. 111–126). IEEE. https://doi.org/10.1109/EuroSP.2017.37\n\nDerler, D., Samelin, K., Slamanig, D., & Striecks, C. (2019). Fine-grained and controlled rewriting in blockchains: Chameleon-hashing gone attribute-based. In *Network and Distributed System Security Symposium (NDSS 2019)*. Internet Society. https://doi.org/10.14722/ndss.2019.23066\n\nDeuber, D., Magri, B., & Thyagarajan, S. A. K. (2019). Redactable blockchain in the permissionless setting. In *2019 IEEE Symposium on Security and Privacy (SP)* (pp. 124–138). IEEE. https://doi.org/10.1109/SP.2019.00039\n\nJohnson, R., Molnar, D., Song, D., & Wagner, D. (2002). Homomorphic signature schemes. In B. Preneel (Ed.), *Topics in Cryptology – CT-RSA 2002* (LNCS 2271, pp. 244–262). Springer. https://doi.org/10.1007/3-540-45760-7_17\n\nKrawczyk, H., & Rabin, T. (1998). *Chameleon hashing and signatures* (Cryptology ePrint Archive, Paper 1998/010; full version of the NDSS 2000 paper). https://eprint.iacr.org/1998/010\n\nPolitou, E., Casino, F., Alepis, E., & Patsakis, C. (2021). Blockchain mutability: Challenges and proposed solutions. *IEEE Transactions on Emerging Topics in Computing, 9*(4), 1972–1986. https://doi.org/10.1109/TETC.2019.2949510\n\nBai, T., Hu', 'why': 'references'}, {'id': 'P49-16', 'file': 'Chapter4_TRF_Model_v1.md', 'old': 'SecuPi. (2025).', 'new': 'OpenNCP Project. (2017). *OpenNCP* [Computer software; GitHub mirror of the epSOS/eHDSI source, commit c8a82d8, 21 April 2017]. GitHub. https://github.com/khypponen/openncp\n\nSecuPi. (2025).', 'why': 'reference'}, {'id': 'P49-17', 'file': 'Chapter4_TRF_Model_v1.md', 'old': 'Scope, N. C., & Rasin, A. (2025).', 'new': 'Sundhedsdatastyrelsen. (2026). *ehdsi* [Computer software; Danish NCPeH deployment of OpenNCP 9.1.0, commit 61094e6, 28 September 2026]. GitHub. https://github.com/Sundhedsdatastyrelsen/ehdsi\n\nScope, N. C., & Rasin, A. (2025).', 'why': 'reference'}]
